WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.339 --> 00:00:16.997
Today we have Peter Reinhardt, he's
the CEO of Segment, went through YC when?

00:00:16.997 --> 00:00:17.717
&gt;&gt; 2011.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:21.419
&gt;&gt; 2011, and
now Segment is doing extremely well.

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:25.291
Peter is going to talk about product
market fit, sort of this magic concept or

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.194
word people use.

00:00:26.194 --> 00:00:28.647
I don't think they really understand it,
certainly,

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:30.510
the first job to get right in any startup.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.370
Peter, thank you very much for
coming to talk to us, and

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:33.630
I look forward to hearing this.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.180
&gt;&gt; My pleasure, all right, so

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.250
I'm going to talk about finding
product market fit today.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.770
Segment is a B2B company,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.950
so that's probably why none
of you have ever heard of us.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.183
But just to give you a little sense,
we're about 150 people, and

00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.260
grew from about 4 people,
3 and a half years ago.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.520
So growing quickly, and

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.640
hopefully I can shed some light on
finding product market fit for you.

00:00:53.668 --> 00:00:56.880
I thought I'd just start by sort of
looking back to Alan K's lecture

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.970
last week.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.750
I think he had some pretty
incredible advice around how to do

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.170
really amazing research by sort
of looking into the future and

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.530
imagining what that future might be like.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.830
And in particular, he had this diagram
of sort of exploring the pink plane,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.770
and sort of testing out
different ideas there.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.820
And then at some point, having a
breakthrough and realizing that, actually,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.092
there's a blue plane, right, and you can
sort of go explore an entirely new space.

00:01:20.092 --> 00:01:21.860
And that's a key breakthrough for

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.900
sort of understanding how you
can really invent that future.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.154
And in particular, he had this idea
of sort of going into the future and

00:01:29.154 --> 00:01:30.380
imaging yourself there.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.210
So he had this Wayne Gretzky idea of
skating to where the puck should be or

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.880
will be, and sort of going there,
grabbing that future,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.720
and then pulling it back to the present.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:44.660
And the way that he talks about doing
this is, I think, amazing for research.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:49.100
He talks about basically going and
sort of imagining that future world.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.620
But I'd actually wager that

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.340
not a single successful company has
actually been founded by doing that.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.376
I think that's, again, awesome for
research, but when it comes to founding

00:01:58.376 --> 00:02:01.458
companies, it actually has nothing to do
with sort of imagining your vision for

00:02:01.458 --> 00:02:03.570
the future and what that future could be.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.347
It's not about sort of how
you wish the world was, but

00:02:06.347 --> 00:02:08.641
it's actually about what customers want.

00:02:08.641 --> 00:02:11.730
And I think a lot of companies
get marketed post facto, of hey,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.170
we have this great idea, and
here's how we deductively logic out what

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.960
customers were going to want,
and then we built the thing.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.920
But in reality, that's not how it
works at all, right, in reality,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.230
it's a very inductive process.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.429
You're going back and
forth with customers, and

00:02:25.429 --> 00:02:27.377
you eventually find something that works.

00:02:27.377 --> 00:02:30.470
And so I wanted to talk a little
bit about that process today, and

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.750
just how critical it is, and
actually, how difficult it is, too.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.530
So one of the most common mistakes that
startups make is to build something that

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.780
no one wants, right, or
solve problems that no one has.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.070
And so that Wayne Gretzky method for
sort of going into the future and

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.550
imagining what that future might look
like, again, is a good idea for research.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:52.940
It might even be a good idea for

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.632
venture capitalists to sort of imagine
what the future is like and sort of put

00:02:56.632 --> 00:03:01.660
startups into different buckets of what
building that future might look like.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.170
But actually, I think it's a really
bad strategy for founders.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.480
And the reason is that most founders can
actually pretty easily build something

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.940
that looks like it's from the future,
or is from the future, to some extent.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.604
It's easy to solve the technology problem
there, but it's actually really difficult.

00:03:18.604 --> 00:03:20.342
That's not really what you need to do,
right,

00:03:20.342 --> 00:03:22.870
you don't need to build something
that is your vision of the future.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.050
You actually need to solve a specific
problem that customers have today,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.790
here, now, and
most startups actually fail to do that.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.350
Most startups actually build something
that looks vaguely futuristic, but is not,

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.270
in fact,
a real problem that people have today.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.420
And it will kill the company every time,
right,

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.230
the market always wins, so we're going to
dig into that a little bit more.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.590
And this sort of problem of finding
product market fit is that 80% of

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.310
all founders fail to
find product market fit.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.980
So four out of five attempts to found a
company just fail at the sort of earliest

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.560
stage of even finding a problem
that you can solve in a unique way.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.990
And the last one out of five are going to
sort of struggle through the remainder of

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:11.490
actually building that company out
from there, but that's just the stats.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.840
I'll share our own story of
finding product market fit.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.070
But the short version is that when
you're not finding product market fit,

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.340
it feels like sort of
a bottomless emotional free fall.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.610
Not to get too dark, but
when you're failing to find it, it sort of

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.378
becomes a strange obsession, and
it could really make you sick, actually.

00:04:30.378 --> 00:04:34.664
To get, maybe, too personal for
a second, in 2012,

00:04:34.664 --> 00:04:39.737
in the midst of our search for
product market fit, I lost ten pounds in

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:44.835
three weeks, and then went to
the hospital twice for panic attacks.

00:04:44.835 --> 00:04:48.862
I haven't had a panic attack before or
since then, and I think it's not just me,

00:04:48.862 --> 00:04:49.465
actually.

00:04:49.465 --> 00:04:52.515
I think Sam, I don't know if I
should show this or not, but

00:04:52.515 --> 00:04:56.377
when he was founding Loot, I think he
basically forgot to eat properly and

00:04:56.377 --> 00:04:58.950
ended up with scurvy,
if I remember correctly.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.160
Sam is back there somewhere.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.510
So basically, be warned, finding product
market fit is, very emotionally, a grind.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.280
And the reason it is is that you really,
really desperately want to find that fit,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.370
right, you really want to figure
out what is going to work.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:17.602
What is something that customers want?

00:05:17.602 --> 00:05:20.770
We're all wired to want to succeed and
help people like that.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.880
And so you're so intent on finding this
thing that you sort of convince yourself

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.810
into seeing mirages of it.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.630
And basically, you convince yourself,
this is the thing, we're almost there,

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.624
it's like this is something
that people really want.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.895
And so this is a picture of myself with
my three co-founders, Elliot, Calvin and

00:05:35.895 --> 00:05:37.930
Ian, in the summer of 2012, I think.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.160
And this is in the midst of
sort of our deepest false hopes

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.812
that we had found something.

00:05:42.812 --> 00:05:44.020
So I'll dive into that story, and

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.030
obviously, I'm here today
because it worked out.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.800
I wouldn't be speaking if we had
just disappeared into the darkness.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.376
But yeah, today we're about 150 people,
and growing rapidly, yeah?

00:05:53.376 --> 00:05:57.301
&gt;&gt; How can you make sure you're
finding a big market, and

00:05:57.301 --> 00:05:59.787
not just a small niche [INAUDIBLE]?

00:05:59.787 --> 00:06:03.251
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good question, so
[COUGH] I think PD's advice on this is

00:06:03.251 --> 00:06:07.121
good and valid, which is that it's
actually harder to find the first problem,

00:06:07.121 --> 00:06:10.610
and solve any problem well,
than it is to find a route out.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:15.347
So finding a route out from some initial
foothold into a broad market is actually

00:06:15.347 --> 00:06:16.275
not that hard.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:20.817
It sounds hard, but once you find any
foothold, you can pretty much find

00:06:20.817 --> 00:06:24.854
something, and I can cover that
a little bit in a bit, but yeah.

00:06:24.854 --> 00:06:28.370
&gt;&gt; Is that because you have more
leverage with the customer?

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:31.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it turns out that once you
solve a problem for a customer,

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:32.990
they'll keep bringing you more problems.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.509
All right, the most efficient thing for
them to do is just say, well,

00:06:35.509 --> 00:06:38.220
I have this other problem that's also
adjacent to it, can you solve that?

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.850
And you're like, sure, and you solve that,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:41.580
and you just keep solving
adjacent problems.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.520
So once you find one thing, it's actually
almost trivial to find the next thing, and

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.440
the next thing, and the next thing,
especially in selling to other businesses,

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.080
where you have someone who
can just tell you more stuff.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.701
Much harder in consumer,

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:59.131
I'm the wrong guy to talk to you
about consumer stuff, I don't get it.

00:06:59.131 --> 00:07:02.108
Cool, but back then,
we were just four dudes in an apartment,

00:07:02.108 --> 00:07:04.200
we were failing to find
product market fit.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.685
We were convincing ourselves that maybe we
had it, or we were on the cusp of it, and

00:07:08.685 --> 00:07:10.311
we were writing a lot of code.

00:07:10.311 --> 00:07:12.295
I saw someone back there who
was writing code earlier.

00:07:12.295 --> 00:07:16.220
We were writing a lot of code,
and we had no customers.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:20.430
That's the wrong order of operations, and
so we were tricking ourselves into this.

00:07:21.782 --> 00:07:24.230
In reality,
the good thing that was going on here,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.190
the reality was that we were
a bunch of cockroaches.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.248
And the reason is that
it's very important,

00:07:30.248 --> 00:07:34.940
pre-product market fit, to basically
save as much cash as possible,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:39.340
spend as little as possible and
extend your runway as long as possible.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:43.900
So a lot of founders make a mistake of
spending a bunch on sales, or marketing,

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.628
or other things as soon
as they have an idea.

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:50.480
But in reality, until you find product
market fit, until you find something

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:53.120
that people want, you shouldn't
be spending any time on that.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.160
You should just be spending time
talking with customers and iterating.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.140
And you really want to constrain your
runway, or constrain your burn so

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:00.290
that you can have a really long runway.

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:02.280
So that's what we did,
we kept our burn really low.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.620
I think we paid ourselves like,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.670
the minimum allowable amount by law,
which was 20 or 30k a year.

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:12.940
We lived in our apartment/office and
like the company paid for

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:16.120
part of it as an office,
as it was the minimal thing.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.520
And we stretched out our runway
through as many ideas as we could.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:23.970
And so to sort of make this process a
little bit more explicit, step one is that

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.590
you build, launch, and sort of
iterate on several different ideas.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.160
And this is where it's really
important to be a cockroach,

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.430
where you're sort of conserving
everything you have.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:36.040
Then suddenly something magical happens,
we'll talk about that.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:39.310
And this is product/market fit and
it really does feel magical.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.410
Hopefully, I can illustrate to you and
help you feel that.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.410
And then,
three product/market fit, sort of,

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:50.710
suddenly turns you into
a unicorn/cockroach.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:51.970
And rather than just surviving,

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.990
after product/market fit,
everything gets suddenly easier, right?

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:59.020
You can, not easy but easier.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:01.860
Customer show up and buy your thing,
people want to join your team.

00:09:03.305 --> 00:09:05.580
You're still pushing
the boulder uphill if you will,

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.730
but you're not totally
constrained on progress, right?

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.390
You can actually feel forward momentum for
the first time, it's not like

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:15.400
one step forward at the product idea, full
step back when a customer doesn't care,

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.950
you actually can keep iterating on
a product that's starting to work.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.740
So, let's take that middle portion and
deconstruct it a little bit.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.970
First we are going to deconstruct
what we call category leaders.

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.540
These are really large companies
that have been very successful.

00:09:29.832 --> 00:09:33.340
Then we'll get to the heart of
why product market fit is so

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:34.500
important at the very beginning.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.010
And we'll talk about
what bad fit feels like.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:38.840
Bad product market fit.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.210
And then we'll talk about what good
product market fit feels like.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:44.320
And we'll try to do that through
stories because the goal here is to for

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:45.900
you to walk away and

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.950
be able to more easily identify
which is which, with your own ideas.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:52.560
So category leaders.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.910
The reason we care about category
leaders is that they're much,

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:58.230
much larger than basically the rest
of the companies out there.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:09:59.050
So I'll give you an example.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.770
Amazon and Facebook are sort of consumer
companies that you might be familiar with.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.550
Salesforce is a company that sells
to other businesses that provide

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:08.680
sales software basically.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.800
And the reason that we're interested
in them is that they're huge, right.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:13.840
They're often ten or

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:17.250
a thousand times larger than
the next competitor in the space.

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:22.310
So, Salesforce for example you've
probably heard of SugarCRM and

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.750
Zoho, are probably companies
you've never heard of.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.930
They all do roughly the same thing.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:32.410
And so, we want to dig into how
can you build a category leader,

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:33.610
what's the layer underneath that.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:39.880
And basically what it comes down to is
building a platform, where it's not

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:44.170
just a product that you're selling, but
where the data inside of your product

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:48.090
is actually useful for other businesses
to build their business on top of yours.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.500
And that's what Salesforce has done,
that's what Amazon has done,

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.390
that's what Facebook has done.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:55.510
If you look at Salesforce, they have
something called the appexchange, and

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:59.330
the appexchange sort of reveals all
of the data inside of Salesforce and

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.940
allows other companies to build marketing
and sales products on top of that.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.260
Similarly, Amazon has
the reseller program, right?

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:11.290
Where now there's ton of other businesses,
built on top of Amazon.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:15.830
And so, the key here is having some sort
of platform, eventually, when you get

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:19.200
the scale having some sort of platform
that other companies are building on.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.710
Selling, and by nature that pulls
people into your ecosystem.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.770
But, how do you build
a platform like that?

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.070
I know Peter Thiel may not be
the most popular right now, but

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:34.130
his opinion is to build a sustainable and
compelling platform.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.810
You really need to get to
100 million in revenue.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:40.030
And the reason is just that you need to
be at a scale, where someone actually can

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.610
capture a couple percentage
points of your customer base and

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.450
build a real business themselves.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.640
So if they can capture a couple
percentage points, say, 2 or

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.090
3 million in revenue,
that's a real business.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:53.300
You can build something on top of that.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.350
So the sequence I weigh here,
you want to build a category leader,

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:58.780
to do that you need a platform, to do
that you need a 100 million in revenue.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:11:59.530
So let's keep digging.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.060
100 million in revenue is tough, right?

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:02.500
So, let's keep digging down.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.960
I think you met Jason Lumpkin
a couple of weeks ago, am I right?

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:09.335
Maybe he is coming soon.

00:12:09.335 --> 00:12:14.220
He's written some amazing stuff about
building software as a service businesses,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.900
would highly recommend reading
all of his answers on Quora.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.920
And he basically breaks down the path
to 100 million in revenue in sort

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:22.140
of three different steps.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:27.960
He says zero to one million is
impossible $1-10M is improbable and

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:28.660
$10-100M is inevitable.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:32.840
And he says the $10-100M is inevitable,

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:35.340
because at that point there is so
much momentum.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.580
You have customers out there who
are singing your praises, they're buying,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:40.720
their companies are going to buy more.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.250
And it might take you a while, but

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:43.894
eventually you're going to
get from $10-100M.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:49.140
One to ten million I think is
basically always a brutal grind for

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:52.600
the founders, and the reason is that
you're running a real business.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:53.660
You have real customers.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.250
You have some scale.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:58.430
Your customers have reasonable
demands about sort of how,

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.650
the quality of service that
they expect from the product.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.570
But the problem is that
pre-ten million in revenue,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:07.010
you have a really tough time attracting
a world class management team.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:08.970
because you can't hire
a great exec team yet

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:10.990
because the large enough
market isn't there.

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.530
And so, what you have is basically all the
early crew sort of like holding the ship

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:16.590
together and trying to make
sure that it's going to work.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.550
So, that's a very tough period, I think,
for most founders to go through.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:23.006
And then, zero to one million
isn't possible because this is

00:13:23.006 --> 00:13:27.465
finding market product fit and 80% or
more of people fail at that first step.

00:13:30.136 --> 00:13:33.995
And so the question is, how can you become
not and how can you actually make it

00:13:33.995 --> 00:13:37.810
through that impossible section and
become one of the ones that succeeds?

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.714
And one of the things that people
talk about as investors or

00:13:40.714 --> 00:13:43.451
founders is how important it
is to learn from failure.

00:13:46.002 --> 00:13:50.510
And I recently read a pretty good study
talking about the stats of, sort of,

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:53.962
the actual analysis of failure and
success in startups and

00:13:53.962 --> 00:13:55.740
finding product market fit.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:14:01.190
And basically the research shows that
you're actually no more likely to succeed

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:03.770
the second time around if
you failed the first time.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.150
So if you fail in finding product
market fit the first time,

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.580
your odds of success are still 20 or
22% the second time around.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:15.150
But, if you succeed in finding product
market fit, the chances of your success

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:20.220
the second time around go from 22%
to 34% which is still miserable.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.730
But, it's at least 50% better.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:24.400
And so what I think that means is
there's actually not that much

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.980
information encoded in failing
to find product market fit.

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:30.110
Like you don't actually worry that much.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.538
But it's actually quite a bit encoded
in feeling the success of actually

00:14:33.538 --> 00:14:35.010
understanding what did work.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:42.395
And so when we were struggling to find
products market fit in 2011 and 2012,

00:14:42.395 --> 00:14:43.545
I felt this really acutely.

00:14:43.545 --> 00:14:46.115
We had failed multiple
times at this point, but

00:14:46.115 --> 00:14:48.945
I still didn't feel like I really
knew what I was looking for.

00:14:48.945 --> 00:14:51.605
What is this mystical
product market fit thing?

00:14:51.605 --> 00:14:52.295
So frustrating.

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:54.815
We kept seeing glimmers of hope.

00:14:54.815 --> 00:14:56.795
We kept convincing ourselves that this,
that or the other thing,

00:14:56.795 --> 00:15:01.075
like a visitor chatting with us on
our site whatever was a big deal, or

00:15:01.075 --> 00:15:04.515
a vaguely interested sales
prospect was product market fit.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:09.070
And so without any positive
product market fit examples,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.940
we didn't really know what it looked like.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.330
And so we could sort of convince
ourselves of everything.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:17.030
And the way I think about it now is I
really desperately if you imagine like

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:21.430
a machine learning model, I had a bunch
of negative training set examples but

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.590
I had no positive training set examples.

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:25.700
And so of course my machine learning
algorithm was like I don't know.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.770
And so I'm not going to
walk through three stories.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:32.550
But I'm going to show you
two examples of failure,

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:36.840
actually five stories, two examples
of failure, and three examples on

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:40.520
the positive side of times that we
actually did feel product market fit.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:43.402
And again, my goal is for you to
actually feel what this feels like so

00:15:43.402 --> 00:15:45.334
that you can identify
it in your own product.

00:15:47.676 --> 00:15:50.996
So today's segment is a customer data
platform, but, we actually started as

00:15:50.996 --> 00:15:54.490
an education tool, and it was actually
designed exactly for lectures like this.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:58.180
So, this is us coding in our Mountain View
apartment, on a summer of 2011.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:02.770
And the idea was that, as a professor
standing up talking up to a class,

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:06.080
you have no idea if anyone in the audience
actually understands what you're saying.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:11.190
And so we are students at that time
at MITA renounce school design.

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:14.440
And we said we really want to do this it
gives students a button to push where

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.510
they can say, I'm confuse, right?

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:18.450
Or I get it, either one.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.890
And the professor would see this graph
overtime of how confuse the students were,

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.220
might be helpful to me right now.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:28.740
And so, we built this.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.780
We were at hundreds of thousands of
lines of code it had like commenting and

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:36.150
notes and all sorts of crazy stuff, and we
actually came to Stanford's campus, we've

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:39.430
convinced them, it might have even been
in this hall Convinced some professors.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.480
We would like run up to them after class.

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:45.950
This is a picture from Berkley, we pounced
on this professor right after class, and

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:47.440
we were testing for
product market fit, right.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.390
We were trying to convince.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.750
Hey professor like did you get
any feedback from your class

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:52.360
during this class?

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:52.990
No.

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:55.210
Okay well we have a solution for you.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:58.290
So, we were hustling to try to get
people to actually use this tool.

00:16:59.818 --> 00:17:04.460
But we we're mostly sort of ignoring any
test of real product market fit there.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:09.140
And so professors would agree to test it
out for a few lectures sort of out of

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:14.640
pity, maybe, for some students
from MIT who were trying to help.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:17.830
And so basically we thought this was
product market fit but it really wasn't.

00:17:17.830 --> 00:17:19.070
And I'll show you why.

00:17:19.070 --> 00:17:20.710
Because if you stand in
the back of the classroom and

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:23.280
look at what people actually
had on their screens,

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.820
none of them were using the product.

00:17:24.820 --> 00:17:26.920
Right, like people were using
all these different things.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:29.210
This is that same class at
Berkley the next week by the way.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:34.570
It was horrifying, and
basically the students,

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:37.050
as soon as students opened their laptops
they all went and did other things.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.290
And so basically putting
a laptop into the classroom was

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.450
the most distracting thing
you could conceivably do.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:46.810
So as you can imagine,
this was pretty horrifying.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.915
One of the more embarrassing
things that could have happened.

00:17:50.915 --> 00:17:55.975
We had just raised 600k coming out of why
Commonator Demo Day, and we had sold this

00:17:55.975 --> 00:17:58.755
vision of like, this is how the future
of classrooms is going to work, right?

00:17:58.755 --> 00:18:04.987
Like, it's going to be digital, it's going
to be online, much as this is a moot, etc.

00:18:04.987 --> 00:18:09.680
And it was a great vision, but
again the market wins every time.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.250
It doesn't matter where vision is,
it matters what the market actually wants.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.590
And in this case,
the students didn't care.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:17.220
All right, the students didn't actually
get that much value out of using the tool.

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:21.790
And actually if you go back,
we should have had an even earlier warning

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.550
sign which was that the professors didn't
really want to use the tool either.

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:25.140
Right?

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:26.400
I mean you go and talk to the professors,

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.040
they would sort of out of pity agree
to test it for a few lectures.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:32.910
But that is not the same thing as
product market fit where they're like

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:35.300
holy crap that solves
this problem that I have.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:41.590
Bullying customers into using your
product is not anything close to

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:43.970
product market fit,
even if they reticently agree to do it.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:47.830
I think that being dismissive of users and
having your

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:51.160
clear vision of the future that isn't
necessarily solving a problem for

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:55.780
your customers is a pretty stunning
failure on our part, and is a key thing

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.980
that founders do again and again and again
in their search for product market fit.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.010
So then we had to the awkward thing.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:08.150
Which by the way is the right thing of
calling back all the investors and saying.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:10.180
This was like four weeks after
they sent the checks right.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:15.050
By the way, turns out this is a terrible
idea we are going to do something else.

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:17.410
Do you want your money back?

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.760
And in most of the cases
the investors did take.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:22.030
Or sorry, did not take the money back.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:24.940
Instead, we invested for the team,
like go find another idea.

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:26.670
We believe in you guys.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.220
Go find something else.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:31.970
So he said, okay, let's do it.

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:35.520
And we were all very committed to working
together as team or four founders.

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:38.630
So we shut down the lecture tool.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:41.370
We went and sort of shut down all
the classrooms, and then we went back to

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.960
the whiteboard and we said, what is
something that is sort of interesting

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:48.940
here and we had always felt like we should
have been able to determine that we didn't

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:53.000
have product market fit, that the product
usage wasn't there from our actual data.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:55.700
The way that we actually figured this out,
right?

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:59.490
Was we went and we stood in the classroom,
back where Sam is.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:02.100
And looked at what was on
all the laptop screens.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.269
And that was how we figured out whether
we had product market fit or not.

00:20:05.269 --> 00:20:07.070
But we should have been able
to do that with the data.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:08.980
We should have been able to
just look at the analytics and

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:10.850
figure out not only are people using it or

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:15.780
not, but are anthropology classes using it
different than computer science classes?

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.870
And so we decided to build
basically an analytics tool,

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:25.810
which it turns out as bad idea,
in case anyone was considering that.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:29.630
And so the way that we approach this is,

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:31.420
how many of you have read
the book Lean Start Up?

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:35.700
Wow, that's actually awesome.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:36.804
You all are way far ahead.

00:20:36.804 --> 00:20:40.410
So as talks about you want to
get out of the building and

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:41.530
actually talk to customers, right.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.570
So we read that right around this
moment and we're like, okay,

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:45.890
we're going to go out and
talk to customers.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:50.290
So we did that, and
we tried to validate our ideas.

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:53.330
So we'd take people out for coffee and
we'd pull people from companies and

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:57.300
try to figure out if they were
interested in analytics products.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:00.818
And I'd say again and again,
they were vaguely interested, right?

00:21:00.818 --> 00:21:02.580
And they had willingness to meet and
chat with us.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:04.890
And they said they wanted product
updates as things came out.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:08.360
And so we thought this must be it, right?

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:09.520
This must be product-market fit.

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:10.970
People are interested in what we're doing.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.070
Again, this is not product-market fit.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.060
This is idle interest.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:21.900
Very, very big difference and so based
on this sort of we got very excited and

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.930
we spend about the next six
months just writing codes and

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:28.580
now this is in a new office, that's my
co-founder as you can see there's lots of

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:33.410
code on our screen again,
wrong order of operations.

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:37.620
I had gone on one sales trip
to visit potential customers.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.980
They were all pretty happy with mixed
panel and Google Analytics, but

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:45.290
that had these edge case features that
they were hoping that we could solve.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:47.650
So, I sort of tricked myself into
believing that these little edge cases

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:49.310
that we might be able to solve were

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.160
actually a really wide gap
we could fill as a product.

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.350
And so I came back, and
I would sort of pitch my cofounders, and

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.820
we would keep sort of believing
that we were almost there.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:59.580
If we just ship one more feature,
one more thing,

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:01.410
we're going to get to product market fit.

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:04.909
Again, bad idea.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:10.130
Give you another one, we used lots of
little positive interactions like this.

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:14.020
You can also read this not as a positive
interaction, but every once in a while,

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:17.000
some stray person would visit our website,
and they'd open a chat.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:18.670
This is idle interest.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:19.850
Hey, what segment?

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:24.890
And there's a complete
transcript of that website chat.

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.270
You can see it's 3:00AM,
so we're up late coding.

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:30.240
And, yeah, this person is interested.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.050
So maybe this is product market fit.

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:42.640
Again this is not what it looks
like this is ideal interest.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:45.470
So it's now December 2012,
we've been at this for a year and

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:48.600
a half and
we decided that something was wrong.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:51.440
Right, clearly this was not working.

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:53.810
And so we went back to YC, we emailed.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.030
A Paul Graham.

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:56.370
And we said.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:57.960
Hey, we think we should catch up and

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:59.940
sort of explain what's happened
over the last year and a half.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:00.970
And so, okay, great.

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:01.720
So we go.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:04.030
This is us in front of
the old YC building, and

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.930
we're walking around the little
cul de sac by YC on Pioneer Way.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:08.930
We bring him up to speed.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:10.550
Okay, we've spent half a million dollars.

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:13.710
Here's everything we've been through
over the last couple of years.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:15.360
And as we walk around, he comes to a stop.

00:23:16.545 --> 00:23:20.430
Aand looks at us, and he says, "so,
just to be clear, we spent half a million

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:26.320
dollars and you have nothing to show for
it." totally accurate.

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:31.720
And super fair and sort of like shocked us
into, ****, yea, we gotta do something.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:36.449
And that was the moment where we
realized we had hit rock bottom.

00:23:36.449 --> 00:23:39.520
But we still have a 100k left so
we still got one more shot, right?

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:41.580
Alright, so that was all
the product market failure cases,

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.810
now you're going to see some successes.

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:44.350
But let's rewind.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:47.210
Let's go all way back to
the first week of YC.

00:23:48.395 --> 00:23:52.445
And in that first week, we have been
like well, we have this class and

00:23:52.445 --> 00:23:55.675
lecture tool and
we should have analytics on it, right?

00:23:55.675 --> 00:23:59.545
So, we looked at the different
analytics tools, guest metrics,

00:23:59.545 --> 00:24:01.210
Google analytics, mixed panel.

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:03.980
And we were looking at
what's similar here and

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:07.010
we saw basically they have different
graphs and different APIs.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:10.130
But it's actually the same data going
into all these tools it's just that

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:12.520
they give you different
stuff out the other end.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.130
And we were like, well, we don't really
want to make a business decision here

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:17.780
about which tool we want to use.

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:20.400
So we'll just solve the engineering
problem because we're engineers and

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.600
we'll just build some code that
sends data to all three and

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:24.030
does this automatic translation.

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:25.250
So we put one data point in,

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:27.950
gets translated into three API calls
that go out to all three services.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:32.790
Cool, this was like 100 lines of code
in the hundreds of thousands that

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:33.790
we wrote, right?

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:35.228
Set that aside.

00:24:35.228 --> 00:24:38.330
And then now four months later, it gets
improved a little bit, four months later,

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:39.860
it gets improved a little bit.

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:43.010
At that point we are trying to sell
our own analytics tool, right?

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:44.490
Akin to Mixpanel and Google Analytics.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:48.460
And we keep encountering the sales
objection when we're trying to sell it,

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:51.460
which is I already have
Mixpanel installed and

00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:53.996
I don't really want to install your
tools it seems like a lot of work.

00:24:53.996 --> 00:24:58.940
So my co-founder Ilya has this great idea,
he says, what if we take that little

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:02.520
library we wrote a year ago that
we've totally forgotten about and

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:05.990
we add ourselves as the fourth
service that it can send data to.

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:08.760
And then every time someone hits us with
that objection, we hit them back with

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:12.710
the open source library and we say, okay,
great now you can try both in parallel.

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.160
And we use this like a growth hack,

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:18.470
basically, to get customers
to start adopting our tool.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:21.300
Okay great, we start doing that, start
setting it up, people start replying.

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:23.610
This is awesome.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.440
I love the library,
I'm definitely going to use it.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:28.080
A few weeks later we follow up.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:31.490
Hey, we saw you're not still sending any
data to segment.io, what's going on?

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.910
And they're like well,
the library is fantastic.

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:35.398
I just don't really want to
use your analytics service.

00:25:37.755 --> 00:25:39.340
Should have taken note right then.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:43.150
But a few months go by,
people start storing this on GitHub.

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:46.080
Maybe it gets up to, it was a big deal for
us at the time, like 30 stars.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:49.210
And I think there was maybe
one pull request issued.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:54.230
And then, fast forward some more,
people keep sort of paying attention.

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:56.300
It's the first time we'd ever felt pull.

00:25:56.300 --> 00:25:59.820
People were just finding this thing and
doing something with it.

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:04.337
And so fast forward,
we have this conversation with PG, and

00:26:04.337 --> 00:26:10.106
the next day we sit down and we're like
all right, we need a new idea, right?

00:26:10.106 --> 00:26:13.830
And so my co-founder Ian,
he's like, you know what?

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:14.700
I have an idea,

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:19.250
you remember that analytics JS library
that has been sort of idling on GitHub.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:20.530
I think that could be a big business.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.390
And I was like, you've got to be kidding
me, that's the worst idea I've ever heard.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:29.010
First of all, it's open source, and
second of all, it's 580 lines of code so

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:30.420
who the heck's going to pay for that?

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.130
Right, how do you build a business
around that, it makes no sense.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:35.850
And so we were like fighting and
fighting and fighting and I went home and

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:38.590
I was wracking my brains so
like, how can I kill this idea?

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:39.110
It's really bad.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:42.040
And it's going to sink us,
we only have one more shot.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.310
And then, so I came in the next day and
I was like, all right,

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:46.790
guys, here's what we're going to do.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:48.000
We're going to build a landing page.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:49.150
It's going to be awesome landing page.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:50.000
It's going to be beautiful.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:51.620
We're going to put it up on Hacker News.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:55.310
It's going to push the product and we'll
have an Email sign up form at the bottom.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:57.230
And we'll use this to just test
whether it's a good idea or not.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:26:59.600
They agree.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:00.590
Okay, great.

00:27:00.590 --> 00:27:02.240
Some I'm like, all right, totally done.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:03.610
We get ready to launch on Hacker News.

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:07.860
I'm starting to think about other
ideas and it goes straight to the top.

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:12.090
So it gets about 300 up votes on Hacker
News, gets a few thousand stars on GitHub.

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:16.000
We have people reaching out to us on
LinkedIn demanding access to the beta.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.140
Like this guys says,

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.050
what does a brother have to do to
get bumped up on your beta list?

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:22.650
And there were others like this, right?

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:25.810
Like holy crap, so full stop, right?

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:29.560
Compare this to everything previously,
everything changed.

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:33.470
This is what product market fail looks
like, where it's not just a single metric,

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:36.850
slowly starts moving,
it's not just a few random conversations

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:39.850
where people express vague interest,
right?

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:42.440
Literally every single
metric went totally haywire.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:46.660
And with our lecture tool and
our analytics tool,

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:51.740
we've been sort of searching in the dark
for what features to build next.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:53.250
We did not have that problem any more,
right?

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:54.940
There were thousands of
people who had signed up.

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:58.898
And they're like, your seven integrations
are good, but I need these ten more.

00:27:58.898 --> 00:28:01.140
I'm like, I"m like deploying it tomorrow,
I'm like blah, blah,

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:02.410
blah, we're like, holy crap.

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:03.360
Like, okay, slow down.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:05.690
And that's one of the key things.

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:09.640
One of the key things is it flips from
being something that you're like pushing

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:13.120
against the customer to all of a sudden
the customer's running away with it.

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:16.690
And you're like, but like hold on,
wait it's not quite ready yet.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.810
And so another example,
with our analytics tool,

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:25.200
we had this sort of sad
unanswered questions and chats.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.750
No one really seemed to care
about what we had built.

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:29.491
But now all of a sudden we
had thousands of stars,

00:28:29.491 --> 00:28:30.820
people were issuing pull requests.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:34.940
We got like ten pull requests in the first
48 hours or something like that.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:40.210
And I guess the other key thins is with
the lecture tool and our analytics tool,

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:41.740
we had had this huge vision, right?

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.550
We have a vision of like here's how
the classroom should operate early.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:46.630
Here's company should do analytics.

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:49.070
And then we went about trying to
build a product that fit that vision.

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:51.880
But this was the total opposite, right?

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:53.950
This is like a little tiny
library that we built for

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:56.610
ourselves that solved the real problem.

00:28:56.610 --> 00:29:00.100
We had no vision associated with
it whatsoever at the beginning.

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:04.210
Now it does because we have something
that we really want to go accomplish.

00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:07.390
But at the beginning, it literally solves
the tiniest of tiny problems and so,

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:08.580
to your question earlier, right?

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:10.642
Like, this is that tiny little foothold.

00:29:10.642 --> 00:29:15.440
And again, it's an open source
library with 580 lines of code.

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:16.800
That's a foothold, right?

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.780
And since then now we've expanded greatly
into doing all sorts of things and

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:22.270
solving adjacent problems for customers.

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:23.060
But the key is that, again,

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:27.440
the market doesn't care at all
what your vision of the world is.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:29.210
The market wants what it wants,
and it will win every time.

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:35.790
So if you walk away with sort of one thing
today, I think it's, let's be incredibly

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:39.600
clear that basically product market fit
doesn't feel like vague idle interest.

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:43.160
It doesn't feel like sort of a glimmer
of hope from some early conversation.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:45.000
It doesn't feel like a trickle
of people signing up.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:50.110
It really feels like sort of everything in
your business has gone totally haywire.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:55.060
There's just this big rush of adrenaline
from customers starting to adopt it, and

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:56.970
sort of ripping it out of your hands.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:29:59.850
And it really feels like the market
is dragging you forward.

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:04.010
I think that Dropbox founders
said this best actually,

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:06.680
that product market fit feels
like stepping on a landmine.

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:10.610
And you really, you can't mistake the two.

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:13.123
So if you are at all questioning
whether you have product market fit or

00:30:13.123 --> 00:30:13.828
not, you don't.

00:30:17.087 --> 00:30:19.719
So, obviously that didn't stop
us from making this mistake.

00:30:19.719 --> 00:30:27.031
So just to make sure that this
is [INAUDIBLE], no, all right.

00:30:27.031 --> 00:30:31.140
So, I thought ClassMetric for
sure had product market fit, right?

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:32.140
Big vision.

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:33.330
Market said no.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.440
Like, market doesn't care what you think.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:38.882
I thought Segment.io for
sure had great product market fit.

00:30:38.882 --> 00:30:42.640
Again market said no,
market doesn't care, market wins.

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:46.102
And even on our third attempt when we did
find a product market fit, I thought for

00:30:46.102 --> 00:30:48.600
sure they were like,
this is too tiny to matter, right?

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:51.412
But actually it solved the real problem,
and

00:30:51.412 --> 00:30:55.197
the market demanded it and
sort of ripped it out of our hands.

00:30:55.197 --> 00:30:57.846
Either that goes to show
sort of how obtuse I am or

00:30:57.846 --> 00:31:00.712
how hard it is to actually
find product market fit.

00:31:00.712 --> 00:31:04.031
&gt;&gt; So how come people for
something that was open source and

00:31:04.031 --> 00:31:05.518
only a couple lines of code?

00:31:05.518 --> 00:31:06.970
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:31:06.970 --> 00:31:08.755
So it turns out that
the open source library by-

00:31:08.755 --> 00:31:10.460
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Repeat the question.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:14.470
&gt;&gt; Sorry, the question is why
would someone pay anything for

00:31:14.470 --> 00:31:15.930
an open source library
that's 580 lines of code?

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.350
It's a good question.

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:21.580
It turns out that the open
source library by itself

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:24.190
doesn't totally solve the problem.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:28.251
So the actual problem that we found out
that we were solving after we launched it

00:31:28.251 --> 00:31:31.821
was that marketing teams keep coming
to engineering departments and

00:31:31.821 --> 00:31:35.881
saying I just signed a contract with
a exact target in marketing tool or

00:31:35.881 --> 00:31:38.800
I just a contract with W Analytics and
here's the docs.

00:31:40.240 --> 00:31:43.690
And engineering team says,
what the heck, I have a road map.

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:45.528
Like, I need to be
executing this whole thing.

00:31:45.528 --> 00:31:48.220
I can't do this analytics implementation.

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:53.080
And so what really needed to happen and
sort of what analytics suggest

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:56.030
could solve the problem, that we could
solve the hosted version it was allowing

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:59.450
engineers to do a single implementation
of collecting that data once.

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:01.810
And then letting marketers just
push buttons in our interface and

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:03.480
send the data wherever they need it.

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:05.900
But the open source library
doesn't quite solve that problem.

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:08.580
Engineers saw that it was
the right abstraction, but

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:10.840
if a marketer needed a new tool and
there is an open source version,

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:13.360
the engineer still has to go compile it.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:14.975
So, in some ways we got lucky,

00:32:14.975 --> 00:32:18.342
that the open source version
doesn't fully solve the problem.

00:32:21.297 --> 00:32:25.370
Cool, so I'm going to steal gratuitously
from Allen K's slides again.

00:32:25.370 --> 00:32:30.208
And I think finding product market fit
feels like finding the sort of blue plane,

00:32:30.208 --> 00:32:32.140
when you're in the pink plane.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.699
And I want to give some other examples
of finding product market fit,

00:32:35.699 --> 00:32:38.270
after finding that initial
product market fit.

00:32:38.270 --> 00:32:41.481
And so the key difference from that
initial breakthrough from what

00:32:41.481 --> 00:32:45.551
Alan Kay was talking about, is that rather
than removing yourself from the world and

00:32:45.551 --> 00:32:48.873
trying to imagine what the future is like,
you actually need to go out

00:32:48.873 --> 00:32:52.860
into the world and
research what problems people really have.

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:56.770
But then once you find that product,
once you find that sort of blue plain,

00:32:56.770 --> 00:33:00.680
then things actually
get enormously easier.

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:03.820
That blue plane is basically a foothold
into a totally new perspective.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:07.950
And I think Allen K talked about
the value of perspective and context.

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:10.470
sort of get this foothold into a whole
new way of thinking about the world, and

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:12.590
new way of solving this problem for
someone.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:16.220
And so, not only do you know
what good fit feels, like but

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.470
you are now operating in
sort of green pastures.

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:22.605
And you are approaching problems in
ways that all the encumbrance and

00:33:22.605 --> 00:33:24.375
the pink plan don't think
about in the world.

00:33:26.134 --> 00:33:30.670
So I wanted to give you two examples
of finding product market fit,

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:33.450
since that initial a win for us.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:36.400
A short intro to what Segment does,

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.230
we basically help you collect data
from mobile apps and websites,

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:41.970
we pull it up to Segment and we fan it out
to all the different tools that you need.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:46.080
And, that's all you really need to know.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:49.790
One of the places we could
send data was Amazon S3, so

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:52.120
this is basically just a place
to put all of your log files.

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:57.109
And we started to notice that all
of our business to customers,

00:33:57.109 --> 00:33:58.632
were using this one integration.

00:33:58.632 --> 00:34:00.968
They were all sending their data to S3,

00:34:00.968 --> 00:34:03.520
like you have to do
something with the data.

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.816
You don't just like collect log files,
do something with the log file and

00:34:06.816 --> 00:34:08.799
you're like what the heck
is going on here.

00:34:08.799 --> 00:34:12.922
And so we went and visited five of our
largest customers in New York about three

00:34:12.922 --> 00:34:16.538
years ago, and we said okay,
you're using this integration, but

00:34:16.538 --> 00:34:19.150
what the heck are using it for?

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:22.890
And for five out of five customers
in a row they said "well,

00:34:22.890 --> 00:34:26.440
we have a data engineering team"
that's taking data from the S3 bucket,

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:29.410
converting it into CSV files and
managing all the schema translation, and

00:34:29.410 --> 00:34:31.675
then uploading it into a data warehouse.

00:34:31.675 --> 00:34:32.905
Red shift.

00:34:32.905 --> 00:34:34.675
And the first time I heard that
from a customer I was like, okay,

00:34:34.675 --> 00:34:36.255
that's interesting and I took a note.

00:34:36.255 --> 00:34:38.835
Went to the second meeting,
customer said exactly the same thing.

00:34:38.835 --> 00:34:40.965
I was like, that's weird,
okay, take a note.

00:34:40.965 --> 00:34:43.255
Third conversation I was like,
all right, this is getting ridiculous.

00:34:43.255 --> 00:34:46.025
Did you guys talk ahead of time or
what happened here?

00:34:46.025 --> 00:34:49.245
And by the fifth time it's like, okay,
well obviously I know what we need to

00:34:49.245 --> 00:34:53.005
build, we need to build a connection
from this straight to red shift.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:59.167
So then the question is,
we went and built that.

00:34:59.167 --> 00:35:00.630
And just to show you now,

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:04.752
now we have a company that axis here
in millions of dollars in revenue.

00:35:04.752 --> 00:35:10.370
And so we have a real product
that real people are using.

00:35:10.370 --> 00:35:12.960
And so what does product market
fit look like at that point?

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:14.790
So we're introducing
a second product basically.

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:17.360
Well, it looks like this.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.930
So you can tell when we
introduced red shift.

00:35:19.930 --> 00:35:24.400
And basically again, almost every
metric in the business goes nuts.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:26.680
So, it's very, very clear whether or
not you have something,

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:29.310
that is really transformative for
your customers or not.

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:35.010
One more story and
then I will open it up for Q and A.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:41.750
So, this is maybe about five months ago,
we had

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:45.430
five ideas for products that we thought
might be exciting to our customers.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:51.340
So I went to visit the customer up a large
company, up in the Pacific Northwest.

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:55.060
And, I sat down with a data architect
there and I said okay, here are the five

00:35:55.060 --> 00:35:58.610
ideas that I want to run through and
see if they're interesting to you.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:03.610
So I went through the first idea and
he was like, yeah, you know what?

00:36:03.610 --> 00:36:04.420
I totally get it.

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:06.240
That sounds super valuable.

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:08.243
And I went through the second one and
he's like, that's really cool.

00:36:08.243 --> 00:36:09.067
That's cool that you can do that.

00:36:09.067 --> 00:36:11.249
And like, that makes a lot of sense.

00:36:11.249 --> 00:36:14.820
And then third idea,
fourth idea, same kind of thing.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:17.620
And so this is how I would summarize that,
that's great,

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:19.320
I totally understand what the value is.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:22.144
It means, yeah, that would be great.

00:36:22.144 --> 00:36:26.788
Doesn't care is what that is code for,
that's someone being nice.

00:36:26.788 --> 00:36:29.491
And then on the fifth idea I said,
hey, here's what we can do,

00:36:29.491 --> 00:36:30.720
blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.666
And he said "wait sorry, you can do what"?

00:36:34.666 --> 00:36:37.719
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I re-explained it and he said,

00:36:37.719 --> 00:36:39.260
" interesting".

00:36:39.260 --> 00:36:41.990
He turns to his friend and says can you
set up a follow up meeting with this team,

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:43.360
this team and this team.

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:48.750
We also need to tell Joe about this,
because it could affect this other thing.

00:36:48.750 --> 00:36:52.110
That is the feeling of product market fit,
which is like, you're like wait, wait,

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:55.280
wait, no, no, no it doesn't exist yet,
these are ideas we might

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:58.166
like all of a sudden the customer
is just going to rip it out of you.

00:36:58.166 --> 00:37:01.231
And so now you're on a tight timeline
because the customer expects that it

00:37:01.231 --> 00:37:01.928
already exist.

00:37:03.946 --> 00:37:07.459
So again, that's what product
market fit actually feels like.

00:37:09.320 --> 00:37:13.460
And I think if you want to
find product market fit and

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.540
build one of these category leading
companies, become one of the one out of

00:37:16.540 --> 00:37:18.920
five founders that actually
does succeed in finding this.

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:21.443
I think you just need to be
really honest with yourself.

00:37:21.443 --> 00:37:24.906
That the sort of glimmers of false
hope that you have are not the same,

00:37:24.906 --> 00:37:27.964
as customers actually ripping
something out of your hands.

00:37:27.964 --> 00:37:33.991
And so yeah, you just need to be honest
with yourself, that's the message.

00:37:33.991 --> 00:37:35.013
So, questions?

00:37:37.998 --> 00:37:39.378
Yeah, Sam.

00:37:39.378 --> 00:37:40.304
&gt;&gt; I certainly agree, and

00:37:40.304 --> 00:37:43.047
it's been my experience that that
is what product market fit is like.

00:37:43.047 --> 00:37:47.699
But when you're trying to find it,
[COUGH] how do you even like have for

00:37:47.699 --> 00:37:49.285
what kind of ideas to try?

00:37:52.722 --> 00:37:55.660
When you're just sort of casting
around looking for ideas.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.590
&gt;&gt; I do think that the bigger problem
is not necessarily having the ideas.

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:00.910
I think that everyone has
lots of interesting ideas.

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:03.450
I think the bigger problem is not
killing the bad ideas fast enough.

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:06.860
I think,
actually I have the most respect for

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:09.630
the Codecademy founders in this respect.

00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:13.670
I think they tried 12 ideas in 7 weeks or
something like that in the summer of YC,

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:15.050
something totally absurd.

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:18.580
But they legitimately tried them and
they killed them so fast, and

00:38:18.580 --> 00:38:24.000
the four days before demo day, they
started a new idea which was Codecademy.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:25.055
And then it worked.

00:38:25.055 --> 00:38:28.176
And on Codecademy on launch day,
on demo day they had 300,000 users or

00:38:28.176 --> 00:38:29.095
something like that.

00:38:29.095 --> 00:38:32.891
Where like again, that's the landmine
effect of nothing, nothing, nothing,

00:38:32.891 --> 00:38:36.577
nothing, nothing and they were so
good about killing the ideas that weren't,

00:38:36.577 --> 00:38:39.790
they had no problem throwing out
something that wasn't going to work.

00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:42.300
So yeah, I'm not sure yet.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:45.270
I don't think there's any magic
to finding the right idea.

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:48.540
If you just kill the bad ideas fast
enough, you'll probably find something.

00:38:52.127 --> 00:38:54.796
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Talk a little bit about so

00:38:54.796 --> 00:38:57.424
this seems like
a [INAUDIBLE] lines of code.

00:38:57.424 --> 00:39:00.901
And it's solving a significant but
relatively small, significant but

00:39:00.901 --> 00:39:04.360
small problem, I don't know if
that's the way to describe it.

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:06.090
How do you go about pricing
something like that,

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:08.510
when you're doing that customer discovery?

00:39:08.510 --> 00:39:11.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah so we didn't, the question is
how do you go about pricing something

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:13.530
like this where it's
just 500 lines of code,

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:17.100
it seems like it solves
a small problem for people.

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:18.890
We thought it was a small problem for
people, and

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:22.080
we under priced hugely for a long time.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:24.249
It turns out that that problem that we,

00:39:24.249 --> 00:39:27.680
the 500 lines of code solves
is actually really valuable.

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:31.988
There are, we have lots of customers now
that pay over $100,000 a year to solve

00:39:31.988 --> 00:39:32.763
that problem.

00:39:32.763 --> 00:39:39.660
And there's more to the product now
than the 500 lines of code, a lot more.

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:44.410
But, it's nevertheless is, the size of the
business problem has almost nothing to do

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:45.770
with the amount of code written.

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:51.660
And I think it did take us a little
while to revalue things in that respect.

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:53.400
But to answer your question,

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:56.490
most directly we spent the first
year just accumulating customers.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:58.420
So we just had lots of people adopting it,

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:01.850
we got to maybe 1,000 or 2,000
companies sending data through Segment.

00:40:01.850 --> 00:40:04.592
And then we tried
experimenting with pricing.

00:40:04.592 --> 00:40:08.380
And then I'd say the biggest kick in the
**** we had to actually get to the right

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:10.290
point here is we had a sales advisor.

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:14.730
Who said, there were reasonably
sized companies using us like

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:19.550
Live Nation and Party O and
stuff like that.

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:23.130
And so we would go to sales
meeting at these companies, and

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:25.235
the sales advisor would basically
get me pumped up ahead of time.

00:40:25.235 --> 00:40:28.302
And he'd be like,
Peter you have to ask for $120,000 a year.

00:40:28.302 --> 00:40:31.680
And I'm like, that's crazy,
what are you talking about?

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:34.652
And he's like, Peter,
if you don't ask for $120,000 a year,

00:40:34.652 --> 00:40:36.223
I'm quitting as your sales advisor.

00:40:36.223 --> 00:40:40.650
It's like, well all right,
here we go, right?

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:42.406
And so I would ask, I'd turn beet red and

00:40:42.406 --> 00:40:44.583
then they would negotiate
it down to 18k a year.

00:40:44.583 --> 00:40:46.883
But if you keep doing it,
eventually someone is like,

00:40:46.883 --> 00:40:48.488
well that seems reasonable, and so.

00:40:48.488 --> 00:40:50.872
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:40:50.872 --> 00:40:54.309
you have to keep testing
the value like that, right?

00:40:54.309 --> 00:40:57.130
because you don't know how much value
you're delivering until you start

00:40:57.130 --> 00:40:57.740
asking for money.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.129
So I think if I had to do it over again I
would start asking for money earlier, and

00:41:02.129 --> 00:41:03.760
I'd be a lot more comfortable with it.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:06.340
If you're solving a real business
problem people are going to be

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:06.860
happy to pay for it.

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:10.715
I'm not sure I totally answered
your question but, yeah

00:41:10.715 --> 00:41:11.474
&gt;&gt; How do you know

00:41:11.474 --> 00:41:14.410
you're asking the right
people about your product?

00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:18.392
What if there should maybe be a just
a small shift in who you should be asking

00:41:18.392 --> 00:41:21.280
that you're not realizing, how did you?

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:22.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the question is,

00:41:22.030 --> 00:41:25.120
how do you know whether you're
asking the right people?

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:28.430
And how do you know whether you should
slightly shift who you're talking to

00:41:28.430 --> 00:41:29.440
versus shift the product?

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:33.690
Yeah, this is actually why it's so hard to
find product market fit the first time.

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:35.310
And why it's so
much easier the second time,

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:36.800
once you have some product market fit.

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:40.842
Because once you do have a defined
customer set that you sell to,

00:41:40.842 --> 00:41:44.252
you'll pretty easily figure out by going
back to those same people whether or

00:41:44.252 --> 00:41:46.524
not you're solving an adjacent problem for
them.

00:41:46.524 --> 00:41:47.699
But its really hard,

00:41:47.699 --> 00:41:52.209
the thing that makes it super hard at the
beginning is its like you have two things.

00:41:52.209 --> 00:41:54.439
And you can either slide the product or

00:41:54.439 --> 00:41:57.795
you can slide the market by
talking to different people.

00:41:57.795 --> 00:42:04.220
And I'm not sure that there's a magic
rule to know when to slide which.

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:07.750
But I think founders get
slippery with themselves,

00:42:07.750 --> 00:42:11.270
they aren't totally honest with themselves
about which thing they're doing when.

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:16.780
So I think it's most important
just to recognize that okay,

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:21.400
if we make this shift, we are shifting
the audience versus shifting the product.

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:24.754
because I think otherwise you
can shift the product and

00:42:24.754 --> 00:42:27.135
the audience simultaneously, and
think you're doing well, but

00:42:27.135 --> 00:42:28.125
then realize you're not.

00:42:28.125 --> 00:42:31.008
So I'm not sure there's a slam-dunk
solution to that, other than just being

00:42:31.008 --> 00:42:33.639
really honest with yourselves about
which thing you're moving when.

00:42:39.178 --> 00:42:39.759
Any other questions?

00:42:44.675 --> 00:42:46.560
Last chance, yeah.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:50.300
&gt;&gt; So you mentioned that you guys
were trying to find proper markets.

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:53.260
That before you guys found it there
was a lot of moments that like

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:54.860
kill this product, kill this product.

00:42:54.860 --> 00:42:59.530
How do you encourage people on your
team to be like let's keep focusing on

00:42:59.530 --> 00:43:01.920
discovering the next thing?

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:05.428
What do you self-talk, and
what do you talk about as a product?

00:43:05.428 --> 00:43:09.799
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think there's a pretty big
difference between finding that first

00:43:09.799 --> 00:43:12.691
product market fit,
where you can kill a product,

00:43:12.691 --> 00:43:15.710
immediately move to
a totally new audience.

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:19.436
Codecademy tried a whole bunch of
different stuff, with a whole bunch of,

00:43:19.436 --> 00:43:23.217
speaking of switching audiences,
they went from S and B, to programmers,

00:43:23.217 --> 00:43:25.422
to [SOUND] restaurants,
all over the place.

00:43:25.422 --> 00:43:29.361
Later on, when you actually are building
a company around it, and you have product

00:43:29.361 --> 00:43:33.260
managers, and each product manager is
sort of searching for a big breakthrough.

00:43:34.600 --> 00:43:37.330
It's a little bit easier in that you
can't shift the audience anymore,

00:43:37.330 --> 00:43:38.400
the audience is fixed.

00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:43.330
And so now it's a slightly more
straightforward problem for

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:46.730
sort of helping a product manager
understand how to go talk to customers.

00:43:46.730 --> 00:43:49.190
And sort of reveal what problems
those customers have, and

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:51.200
then actually solve them.

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:52.850
So it's most training in that loop.

00:43:52.850 --> 00:43:57.130
And it comes from talking to either larger
customers, or your best customers, or

00:43:57.130 --> 00:43:58.440
your worst customers.

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:02.880
And sort of trying to push the boundaries
around the core of very happy people have,

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:04.470
to what other problems you can solve for
them.

00:44:05.530 --> 00:44:06.485
Yeah?

00:44:06.485 --> 00:44:08.960
&gt;&gt; When you talk to your customer,
what do you ask them?

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:09.460
What's your script?

00:44:13.998 --> 00:44:17.086
&gt;&gt; You basically just, the question is,
when you're talking to customers and

00:44:17.086 --> 00:44:20.140
trying to sort of understand what
their problems are, what's the script?

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:23.560
I think the biggest mistake is,

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:29.440
trying to pitch your existing ideas,
there's a place for that.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:31.550
Right, when you have some
product market in fit, and

00:44:31.550 --> 00:44:33.370
you want to test some of this things.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:37.010
But I think most founders, and
certainly we made this mistake early on,

00:44:37.010 --> 00:44:40.180
of pitching and trying to sell.

00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:43.680
Rather that actually trying to
understand what their problems were.

00:44:43.680 --> 00:44:45.571
So now when I talk to a customer,

00:44:45.571 --> 00:44:49.440
I just start by asking what their
business problems are, right?

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:53.605
Hey, person at retail company,
do you spend a lot on Facebook?

00:44:53.605 --> 00:44:55.913
They're like yeah, yeah, yeah,
we spend a lot of Facebook.

00:44:55.913 --> 00:44:59.120
I'm like how do you measure whether
that spend is efficient or not?

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:00.570
And they're like well,
we don't really know.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:02.140
Okay, well is that a problem?

00:45:02.140 --> 00:45:04.007
They're like yeah,
yeah that's a big problem.

00:45:04.007 --> 00:45:05.881
Well okay,
tell me why you haven't solved it.

00:45:05.881 --> 00:45:09.020
And they're like well, we haven't
solved it because blah, blah, blah.

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:11.792
And you just start digging
in to these problems, and

00:45:11.792 --> 00:45:15.071
then you're actually really set
up to do a sale then, right?

00:45:15.071 --> 00:45:17.099
You're like well,
I have just the thing, right?

00:45:17.099 --> 00:45:18.430
And then they're like, holy crap.

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:22.270
But if you don't have a product yet,

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:25.800
now you know exactly what
problem you can fix, right?

00:45:25.800 --> 00:45:31.620
So I think it's more about listening and
digging than it is about pitching an idea.

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:34.740
Yeah?
&gt;&gt; How do you find these customer?

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:39.044
How do you be like hey, this is my
customer, this company or is company?

00:45:39.044 --> 00:45:42.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the question is how
do you find these customers?

00:45:42.250 --> 00:45:46.120
At the beginning, it's hustle,
it's just emailing people cold,

00:45:46.120 --> 00:45:47.920
it's getting interest
through everyone you know.

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:51.149
It's scratching and clawing your
way through your social network and

00:45:51.149 --> 00:45:52.359
through introductions.

00:45:52.359 --> 00:45:56.234
After you have customers in an area,
then it gets easier,

00:45:56.234 --> 00:46:01.550
because you have people coming to you
all the time, mostly if you're inbound.

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:04.519
And so then there's a steady
stream of people to talk to.

00:46:04.519 --> 00:46:08.906
But the initial piece, that's why people
talk about hustle being such an important

00:46:08.906 --> 00:46:11.575
founder quality,
is because no one's going to help you.

00:46:11.575 --> 00:46:14.190
You've just gotta go find them, right?

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:17.730
LinkedIn, investors, friends,
it's whatever you can claw together.

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:21.852
Yeah?
&gt;&gt; So at the beginning,

00:46:21.852 --> 00:46:25.963
when you lectured for these customer,
when you're cold emailing them,

00:46:25.963 --> 00:46:29.540
how many percentage of people
actually come back to you?

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:31.960
The question is,
when you cold emailed customers,

00:46:31.960 --> 00:46:33.360
what percentage of
people come back to you?

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:40.620
I don't remember, that was awhile ago,
very low, single digits percentages maybe.

00:46:42.430 --> 00:46:45.010
You're going to send a lot
emails that are unanswered.

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:45.738
But that's the nature of sales.

00:46:45.738 --> 00:46:48.851
Honestly, if you're in a business
to business kind of environment,

00:46:48.851 --> 00:46:52.040
you're going to send a lot of
emails that go unanswered.

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:56.954
Sales is basically the exercise of getting
the door slammed in your face nine

00:46:56.954 --> 00:46:58.654
times out of ten, right?

00:46:58.654 --> 00:47:00.300
Yeah?

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:03.934
&gt;&gt; What sort of second,
where do you think would be in five years?

00:47:05.285 --> 00:47:07.310
&gt;&gt; Go back to diagram.

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:14.390
So what we have today is basically
sort of stream processing.

00:47:14.390 --> 00:47:17.070
So we have both the ability
to collect data across some

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:18.920
broad section of stuff here.

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:21.800
As well as the ability to fan it
out to a bunch of different places.

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:25.284
And so what we want to do is basically
provide a platform that all of these tools

00:47:25.284 --> 00:47:26.217
can build on top of.

00:47:26.217 --> 00:47:28.440
That's the super short version.

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:31.579
So you can imagine basically every team in
a company needs access to customer data.

00:47:31.579 --> 00:47:35.076
And we want to become the platform
on which those tools are built,

00:47:35.076 --> 00:47:39.352
whether that's email marketing, or
push notifications, or analytics, or

00:47:39.352 --> 00:47:43.730
help desks, or CRMs or
payment systems, fraud detection, etc.

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:47.130
All of those things operate on
a core set of customer data, and

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:50.540
that's the customer data that already
flows through Segment by nature of us

00:47:50.540 --> 00:47:52.580
being that integration connection layer.

00:47:52.580 --> 00:47:55.880
And so we just want to expose that data
to partners to build on top of it.

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:59.178
So when I talked about building
a platform, at the beginning,

00:47:59.178 --> 00:48:00.745
that's the next step for us.

00:48:00.745 --> 00:48:04.134
&gt;&gt; Peter, we are out of time,
thank you very much.

00:48:04.134 --> 00:48:06.055
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

